## Evil

Let:

**E** = a state of affairs, actual or possible

**Actual(E)** = E actually occurs

**Evil(E)** = E is evil

**O(¬E)** = *all things considered*, E ought not occur

**Permit<sub>G</sub>(E)** = God permits E

**G** = a tri-omni God exists

The relevant states of affairs are those where its non-occurrence is logically possible.

> **P1.** Evil(E) ↔ O(¬E)  
>
> **P2.** If G exists, then G is omniscient, omnipotent and omnibenevolent: G knows every actual and possible evil and every consequence of preventing or permitting it; G can prevent any evil when doing so is logically possible; and G never knowingly permits an avoidable state of affairs that, *all things considered*, ought not occur.  
>
> **P3.** G ∧ O(¬E) → O(¬Permit<sub>G</sub>(E))  
>
> **P4.** G ∧ Permit<sub>G</sub>(E) → ¬O(¬Permit<sub>G</sub>(E))  
>
> **P5.** G ∧ Actual(E) ∧ Evil(E) → Permit<sub>G</sub>(E)
>
> ---
>
> **C1.** G ∧ Permit<sub>G</sub>(E) → ¬O(¬E)  
>
> **C2.** G ∧ Actual(E) → ¬Evil(E)  
>
> **C3.** G → ∀E(Actual(E) → ¬Evil(E))  
>
> **∴ ¬G ∨ ¬∃E(Actual(E) ∧ Evil(E))**

## Derivation

Assume:

> **G ∧ Permit<sub>G</sub>(E)**

From **P4**:

> **¬O(¬Permit<sub>G</sub>(E))**

From **P3**, by contraposition, together with G:

> **¬O(¬E)**

Therefore:

> **C1. G ∧ Permit<sub>G</sub>(E) → ¬O(¬E)**

By **P1**:

> **G ∧ Permit<sub>G</sub>(E) → ¬Evil(E)**

Now assume:

> **G ∧ Actual(E)**

Suppose:

> **Evil(E)**

From **P5**:

> **Permit<sub>G</sub>(E)**

But from **C1** and **P1**:

> **¬Evil(E)**

Contradiction.

Therefore:

> **C2. G ∧ Actual(E) → ¬Evil(E)**

Generalising over E:

> **C3. G → ∀E(Actual(E) → ¬Evil(E))**

Hence:

> **∴ ¬G ∨ ¬∃E(Actual(E) ∧ Evil(E))**


## Reasoning:

If E is evil, then:

> **Evil(E) → O(¬E)**

A maximally good agent never knowingly permits an avoidable state of affairs that all things considered ought not occur. An all knowing agent knows every actual evil occurrence and every consequence of preventing it, while an all powerful agent can prevent any evil event when it is logically possible to do so.

Therefore, if a God with these characteristics knowingly permits E, His permitting E must be justified all things considered:

> **G ∧ Permit<sub>G</sub>(E) → ¬O(¬Permit<sub>G</sub>(E))**

But now suppose the theist says both:

> **O(¬E)**

and:

> **¬O(¬Permit<sub>G</sub>(E))**

This creates a problem. If **all things considered**, reality ought to be such that E does not occur, then a perfectly good and omnipotent God ought not permit E. On the other hand, if God's permitting E is permissible, all things considered, then it cannot also be true, in the all-things-considered sense, that E ought not occur.

Therefore:

> **G ∧ Permit<sub>G</sub>(E) → ¬O(¬E)**

And since:

> **Evil(E) ↔ O(¬E)**

it follows for an actual E that:

> **G ∧ Actual(E) → ¬Evil(E)**

Generalising over E:

> **G → ∀E(Actual(E) → ¬Evil(E))**

Hence the theist faces a dilemma:

> ## **¬G ∨ ¬∃E(Actual(E) ∧ Evil(E))**

Either the tri-omni God does not exist or actual evil does not exist in the pro toto sense.

The typical rejoinder is that an evil may be permitted for some greater good (the lessons learned, the ability to appreciate things, free agency, soul-making, downstream goods, the total providential order, etc. etc.). However this does not circumvent the argument. If permitting E is genuinely permissible **all things considered** (even after literally every relevant consideration has been incorporated) then E cannot simultaneously be something that ought not happen. It may be painful or horrific or bad or terrible (etc.) *in some respect*, but it certainly cannot any longer be *evil* in the stipulated pro-toto sense.

> ## “The Holocaust was genuinely evil, but God had morally sufficient reason to permit it because permitting it was necessary for some greater good”.

There are two possibilities.

If the greater good outweighs the reasons against permitting the Holocaust, such that God's permitting it is all-things-considered permissible, then the theist seemingly cannot also say that, all things considered, the Holocaust ought not have occurred.

They can still say:

- it was horrific;
- it involved enormous suffering;
- the perpetrators acted wickedly;
- there were powerful reasons against its occurrence;
- considered in itself, it really ought not occur.

But such statements are now pro tanto or agent-relative claims. They specifically are *not*:

> **O(¬E)**

(in the stipulated pro toto sense).

The theist cannot coherently maintain that an actual event is evil in the all-things-considered sense that it ultimately ought not have occurred, while also maintaining that an omniscient, omnipotent and perfectly good God knowingly and permissibly actualised a world containing it.

For an actual E permitted by G, either:

> **O(¬E)**

in which case God apparently ought not permit E; or God's permission of E is ultimately justified, in which case:

> **¬O(¬E).**

that is to say, that pro toto evil does not exist.


**Greater-good theodicies do not reconcile God with pro-toto evil. They explain why apparently or pro-tanto evil need not be pro-toto evil.**
